canonical_claim.statement:
Printed. PDF p. 5: 'Proposition 2 (ATT Regret). If $\hat\mu_0(x)$ is unbiased and $\pi$ is unconstrained, then the regret for the reward function $w_{\mathrm{ATT}}$ is upper bounded as $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}})\leq\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$.' PDF p. 17: 'Proposition 2 (ATT Regret). Suppose $\Pi$ is the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$. Then the regret for the reward function $w_{ATT}$ is upper bounded by $R(\pi^{w_{ATT}})\leq\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$. Proof. Let $\pi^*=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$. Then $\pi^*(x)=\begin{cases}1&\text{if }\tau(x)>0\\0&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$' and (p. 18) '$R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}})=V(\pi^*)-V(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}})=E[\pi^*(X_i)\tau(X_i)]-E[\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}}(X_i)\tau(X_i)]=E[\tau(X_i)\mathbb 1(\tau(X_i)>0)]-E[\tau(X_i)\mathbb 1(\tau(X_i)>0\cap X_i\in\arg\max_{x\in\mathcal X}\tau(x))]=E[\tau(X_i)\mathbb 1(\tau(X_i)>0\cap X_i\notin\arg\max_{x\in\mathcal X}\tau(x))]$'.

Corrected statement (the bound is kept; the proof no longer uses Proposition 7). In the setting of target P006-T2 for $w_{\mathrm{ATT}}$ (in particular $E[\hat\mu_0(X_i)\mid\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi]=\mu_0(X_i)$ for every $i$ and every $\pi\in\Pi$), for every $n\geq1$ and every feasible set $\Pi$ that contains the policy $\pi\equiv0$ (the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$ does):
(i) $V(\pi)=E[\pi(X_i)\tau(X_i)]=A(\pi)$, and $E[w_{\mathrm{ATT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=h_n(q(\pi))\,V(\pi)$, where $h_n(q)=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(1-q)^j\geq1$ on $[0,1]$;
(ii) the policy $\pi\equiv0$ has expected reward $0$ and welfare $0$;
(iii) every best response $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}}\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E[w_{\mathrm{ATT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$ has expected reward at least $0$, and therefore $V(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}})\geq0$;
(iv) hence $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}})=\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)-V(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{ATT}}})\leq\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$.
The closed form for the regret in the printed proof does not hold for the finite-$n$ best response: in the example of target P006-T1 it equals $\frac9{20}$, while the regret of the unique best response is $\frac1{40}$.

Corrected statement. Let $n\ge1$. Replace Assumption 1 by: Assumption 1' (unbiasedness at the treated evaluation observations): for every policy $\pi$ in the feasible set and every unit $i$, $E[T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))\mid X_i]=0$; equivalently, $E[\hat\mu_0(X_i)\mid X_i,T_i^\pi=1]=\mu_0(X_i)$ on the event $\{\pi(X_i)>0\}$. The estimator $\hat\mu_0$ may be random, and the expectation is taken over it as well. All displayed estimator evaluations are measurable, and Assumption 1' requires $E[|T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))|]<\infty$ for every feasible fixed policy and unit, so its conditional-expectation equality has its ordinary integrable meaning. Under the standing assumptions (S1)-(S3) and (S9) and Assumption 1':
(i) (the identity of Proposition 9) for every $\pi$ in the feasible set, $E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=nE[\pi(X_i)\tau(X_i)]=nV(\pi)$;
(ii) (Proposition 3) for every feasible set $\Pi$, $\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$, so every best response $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}}$ has $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=0$, 'regardless of the feasible set $\Pi$';
(iii) (the rule of Proposition 9) if $\Pi$ is the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$, then $\pi(x)=\mathbb 1(\tau(x)>0)$ is a best response to $w_{\mathrm{TT}}$;
(iv) (a sufficient implementation) if the estimator $\hat\mu_0$, as a jointly measurable random function on $\mathcal X$, is independent of $(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi)$ and $E[\hat\mu_0(x)]=\mu_0(x)$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$ (for example an estimator computed from auxiliary data independent of the treatment population and of the treatment randomization), and $E[|T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))|]<\infty$ for every feasible fixed $\pi$ and every unit $i$, then Assumption 1' holds for every such $\pi$.

